=== K4: frontend/shared verify schema (the comment that names both routes)
/**
 * =============================================================================
 * Verify schemas — shape of /api/verification/verify and /api/documents/:id/verify
 * =============================================================================
 *
 * The verify endpoints return a moderately polymorphic envelope: success and
 * failure paths share most fields, but blockchain / issuer / matchedBy /
 * onChainMetadata appear only in some branches. We model the union as a
 * single permissive object — passthrough() so future fields don't break the
 * frontend, but the load-bearing fields (`verified`, `checks`, `blockchain`)
 * are still validated for type.
 *
 * If the backend changes in a way that drops `verified`, the frontend will
 * see ResponseShapeError instead of a silent `undefined.verified` crash.
 *
 * (T2-F Phase 3.)
 * =============================================================================
 */

import { z } from 'zod';

// Verifier consolidation 2026-04-29 (F-DEMO-VERIFY-01 follow-up):
// Originate is now the single source of truth for /api/verification/verify
// across all envs. Originate emits {documentExists, hashValid, isCertified,
// isRevoked, integrityVerified}. The legacy {exists, notRevoked,
// hasSignatures, blockchainAnchored} fields are kept here so:
//   - the verifier portal UI (which iterates Object.entries(checks))
//     keeps rendering whichever shape arrives
//   - older clients (Gmail addon, third-party verifier scripts) that
//     might still see the legacy shape don't break Zod parse
// `.partial()` + `.passthrough()` make every field optional and unknown
// keys don't fail. Treat this as a permissive union of both shapes
// rather than a strict spec — the load-bearing field is `verified`.
const ChecksSchema = z.object({
  // Originate (production) shape:
  documentExists: z.boolean(),
  hashValid: z.boolean(),
  // KS-563: `isCertified` means an issuer certified the document
  // (status `certified`) and nothing else — it was previously true for
  // merely-anchored documents. `isAnchored` carries chain presence.
